  section = {claims},
  tier    = {code specimen},
  claim   = {"Who wins the last write" PRESUMES two writers --- count the WRITERS before reasoning
             about a race (clobbering, ordering, precedence, a re-apply loop). A presumed race often
             has ONE writer acting on another party's instruction: under the systemd cgroup driver
             kubelet does not write cpu.weight, it sets a systemd PROPERTY over D-Bus and SYSTEMD
             writes the file, so there is one writer and the "last-write race" does not exist --- the
             real question is what that single writer reconciles its config SOURCES to, a different
             mechanism with a different answer. Count how many parties actually PERFORM the write
             (not how many CARE about the value); if one, "who wins" is a category error. The same
             heuristic governs EXECUTION AUTHORITY: neither peer executes, the operator does},
  origin   = {who-wins-the-last-write-presumes-two-writers},
  specimen = {a cpu.weight "race" where a dbus-mediated set collapses to a single writer (systemd), vs a genuine two-writer contest, in concepts.py},
  evidence = {counting parties that CARE about the value sees two (kubelet, the drop-in) and frames a
